(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','DORM MAINTENANCE — To report a leaking faucet or a broken light, submit a ticket at the desk between 8:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. Weekend requests are queued for Monday. For a flooded room or no heat, call the emergency line printed on every floor instead of filing a ticket.','NONE','["Call the emergency line rather than filing a ticket.","Submit a ticket at the desk on Monday.","Wait in line until 6:00 p.m.","Email maintenance after dark."]','A',1,'The notice routes urgent problems such as flooding to the emergency line, not the ticket desk.',16),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the form instruction and answer the question.','CHANGE OF MAJOR FORM — Complete sections 1 and 2, have your current advisor sign box 3, and return the form to the registrar before the last Friday of add/drop. Forms missing the advisor signature go to a pending file and are not processed.','NONE','["It is processed immediately.","It is set aside until an advisor''s signature is added.","It is mailed back to the student.","It stays valid through the next term."]','B',1,'An unsigned form goes to a pending file, so it waits until the signature is added.',17),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','SEMINAR CHAT — Dana: "Bring the photocopied article to Thursday''s seminar; I lost mine." — Theo: "The PDF is in the shared folder, but the library printer is down until Wednesday, so print it early." Dana: "Then I will print at the cafe in the morning."','NONE','["Wednesday, using the library printer","During Thursday''s seminar","Thursday morning, before the seminar","The following Wednesday"]','C',1,'The library printer is down until Wednesday, so Dana plans to print at the cafe on Thursday morning before class.',18),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','GIFT SHOP NOTICE — Shop items may be returned within 14 days with a receipt for a full refund. Books marked final sale and museum reproductions are excluded, and food items are never accepted for hygiene reasons.','NONE','["A printed map returned on day three with the receipt","A scarf returned on day twelve with the receipt","A keychain returned on day fourteen with the receipt","A final-sale book"]','D',1,'Final-sale books are listed as excluded, so they cannot be refunded; the others were within 14 days with a receipt.',19),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the schedule and answer the question.','CAMPUS SHUTTLE — The last shuttle to the train station leaves the union at 11:15 p.m. on weekdays and 9:45 p.m. on weekends. On holidays the weekend timetable applies. The 6:00 a.m. run does not operate on Sundays.','NONE','["9:45 p.m.","11:15 p.m.","6:00 a.m.","There is no evening shuttle."]','A',1,'On weekends, including Sunday, the last shuttle is 9:45 p.m., and the 6:00 a.m. run does not operate on Sunday.',20),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','CAMPUS JOB EMAIL — Thank you for interviewing for the front-desk assistant role. Please reply today with two references and your earliest start date. If we do not hear back by 5:00 p.m., your application moves to the reserve list.','NONE','["Arrange a second interview.","Give the requested details by 5:00 p.m.","Submit a written test score.","Confirm the pay rate."]','B',1,'The email asks for references and a start date by 5:00 p.m. to keep the application active.',21),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','LAUNDRY ROOM — Remove your load within 20 minutes of the cycle ending. Unclaimed items are moved to the shelf by the door and discarded after two weeks. Using a cart or detergent that is not yours is not permitted.','NONE','["Return cleaned clothes to their owner","Discard your detergent after two weeks","A load left at 3:00 p.m. should be taken out by about 3:20","Label every item placed on the shelf"]','C',1,'Loads must leave within 20 minutes, so a cycle ending near 3:00 p.m. should be cleared near 3:20.',22),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','REGISTRAR ALERT — Your enrollment is not confirmed until the tuition hold is released. A hold clears one business day after the bursar records your payment plan. If your schedule still shows a hold tomorrow, call this office; do not re-register for the same courses.','NONE','["A payment plan may need one business day to clear before enrollment is final.","Re-registering removes the hold automatically.","The hold clears the moment a form is filed.","Students must pay the full tuition in cash."]','A',1,'The alert says a hold clears one business day after the payment plan is recorded, so confirmation is not instant.',23),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','QUIET HOURS — On weeknights quiet begins at 11:00 p.m. and ends at 8:00 a.m.; on weekends it begins at 1:00 a.m. Gatherings in the shared lounge are asked to end by the start of quiet time, and hallway noise after that may be reported to the resident advisor.','NONE','["Quiet starts later on weekdays than on weekends","A Sunday lounge gathering should end by 1:00 a.m.","The resident advisor patrols every hour","Weekends have no quiet hours."]','B',1,'Weekend quiet begins at 1:00 a.m., so a Sunday lounge gathering should wrap up by then.',24),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','DIGITAL DESIGN WORKSHOP — Sessions run every Monday at 4:00 p.m. in the learning centre for six weeks. Space is limited to 15; register by Thursday so a seat can be held. Drop-ins are welcome only if fewer than 15 people have signed up.','NONE','["Everyone must attend all six weeks","Registration closes on Monday","The workshop meets twice a week","You may attend without registering if seats are still open."]','D',1,'Drop-ins are allowed when fewer than 15 have registered, so open seats permit an unregistered attendee.',25),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','AUDIO GUIDE — The headset costs $5 and is returned to the desk at exit; it logs the rooms you visit. Groups of ten or more get free headsets when the booking is made at least three days ahead, while individual visitors pay the $5 fee on arrival.','NONE','["A single visitor arriving today","A tour booked a week before the visit for twelve people","Any visitor who returns the headset","A group of eight booked the day before"]','B',1,'Groups of ten or more booking at least three days ahead get free headsets, so the twelve-person tour booked a week ahead qualifies.',26),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','SEMINAR PREP — Before Thursday, read chapter 6 and mark two questions you want discussed. Bring your annotated copy; spares will not be provided. I will open with a ten-minute summary, so arriving late costs you the framing of the discussion.','NONE','["A written essay by Friday","Chapters read aloud in class","A group poster for chapter 6","Preparing specific questions before Thursday."]','D',1,'The email asks each student to mark two discussion questions before Thursday, i.e. to prepare questions in advance.',27),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','TRIP CHAT — Nina: "My train to the conference is delayed forty minutes, so I will miss the nine o''clock panel." — Marco: "The app says the delay grew to an hour, and the organizer now runs the panel until 10:15, so you may still catch part of it."','NONE','["Nina arrives before nine","The panel has been cancelled","The delay grew and the panel now ends later","Marco is also traveling by train."]','C',1,'The app updated the delay to an hour and the organizer extended the panel to 10:15, so both timing facts changed.',28),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','STATION NOTICE — Platform 4 is closed for resurfacing this weekend. Trains that normally leave from 4 now depart from 2 and board ten minutes later than printed. Ticket machines on 2 accept card only during the closure.','NONE','["Buy a paper ticket at machine 4","Board exactly at the printed time","Board the affected train about ten minutes after the printed time","Use platform 4 as usual."]','C',1,'Departures from platform 2 board ten minutes later than printed, so passengers adjust by that margin.',29),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','SHOP CHAT — Clerk: "We can hold your order at the counter for three days; after that it goes back into stock." — Visitor: "I am on the last coach Sunday evening; can I pay on pickup?" Clerk: "Pay online now, or the courier fee is added to the counter price."','NONE','["The item returns to stock if not collected in three days","Payment must be made at the counter on Sunday","The order ships to the visitor''s home","The courier fee is always charged."]','A',1,'The hold lasts three days, then the item goes back into stock, and online payment avoids the courier fee.',30),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','TRANSCRIPT REQUEST — Official copies are released only after all fees are settled. A request placed during a fee hold waits in a queue and is not sent to the address you list. Settle the balance first, then confirm your mailing address on the portal.','NONE','["It is mailed to a wrong address.","It stays queued until the student pays the outstanding fees.","It is cancelled with a refund.","It is released automatically at term end."]','B',1,'Requests made during a fee hold wait and are not sent until the fees are settled.',31),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','CAFE JOB CHAT — Owner: "Your trial shift is Saturday at 7:00 a.m.; please arrive ten minutes early in closed shoes." — Applicant: "I have a family duty until 8:00 that morning; may I start later?" Owner: "The trial runs only one hour, so arriving late means we reschedule to another week."','NONE','["Arriving late pushes the trial to another week.","The shift is shortened to thirty minutes.","The owner hires the applicant on the spot.","Nothing changes; the start time is flexible."]','A',1,'Because the one-hour trial cannot start late, the owner will move it to a different week.',32),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the schedule and answer the question.','MOVE-IN WINDOWS — Hall C assigns arrivals by student number: 000-199 before noon, 200-399 from noon to 4:00, and 400 and up after 4:00. The loading dock closes at 9:00 p.m. no matter the window, so a late group should not plan to unload after dark.','NONE','["The 400-and-up group should finish unloading before 9:00 p.m.","Every group may use the dock until midnight","The noon group must wait until 4:00","Student numbers do not affect timing."]','A',1,'The late window runs after 4:00, but the dock still closes at 9:00 p.m., so that group must unload before dark.',33),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the form and answer the question.','INTERVIEW CHECK-IN — Sign the visitor log and take a number from the rack. Wait in the second-floor lounge; your number will be called, so stay within sight of the display. Numbers unclaimed when called are forfeited and you must re-check in.','NONE','["Pay a visitor fee","Climb to the second floor again","Keep your printed number","Lose your place by not returning promptly when your number appears."]','D',1,'Unclaimed numbers are forfeited and require re-check-in, so leaving the display risks losing your place.',34),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','TOUR CHAT — Guide: "The museum tour starts at 2:00; meet by the clock tower." — Student: "Our bus is stuck in traffic and will be fifteen minutes late." Guide: "Then have the driver drop everyone at the east door; the tour will simply start a little after 2:00."','NONE','["The tour is cancelled","The meeting place moves to the clock tower","The tour begins shortly after its planned time because the bus is late","The students will walk instead."]','C',1,'The guide keeps the east-door drop-off and starts just after 2:00 to absorb the bus delay.',35),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly explain?','Pendulum clocks keep better time than any earlier mechanism, but their accuracy hides a subtle problem: metals expand when warm. A steel rod lengthens as the temperature rises, and because a pendulum''s swing slows as it grows longer, a summer clock loses time that a winter clock did not lose. Around 1726 the English clockmaker John Harrison solved this with the gridiron pendulum, an assembly of alternating brass and steel rods joined to one bob. Brass expands more than steel, so the brass rods push the bob downward while the steel rods pull it upward. Adjusted in the right proportions, the two effects cancel, and the distance from the pivot to the bob stays almost fixed as the room warms or cools. The gridiron did not make clocks perfect, but by trading one kind of error for its opposite it let a household timepiece run for weeks without the daily correction that plainer pendulums demanded.','NONE','["How a combined brass-and-steel design cancels temperature error in pendulum clocks.","Why brass was the favorite metal of eighteenth-century clockmakers.","How John Harrison learned his trade from an English workshop.","Why a pendulum swings faster in a warm room than in a cold one."]','A',1,'The passage names the problem (thermal expansion) and the gridiron solution, in which brass and steel offset each other.',36),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "fixed" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Pendulum clocks keep better time than any earlier mechanism, but their accuracy hides a subtle problem: metals expand when warm. A steel rod lengthens as the temperature rises, and because a pendulum''s swing slows as it grows longer, a summer clock loses time that a winter clock did not lose. Around 1726 the English clockmaker John Harrison solved this with the gridiron pendulum, an assembly of alternating brass and steel rods joined to one bob. Brass expands more than steel, so the brass rods push the bob downward while the steel rods pull it upward. Adjusted in the right proportions, the two effects cancel, and the distance from the pivot to the bob stays almost fixed as the room warms or cools. The gridiron did not make clocks perfect, but by trading one kind of error for its opposite it let a household timepiece run for weeks without the daily correction that plainer pendulums demanded.','NONE','["firmly fastened","unchanging in value","permanently repaired","clearly certain"]','B',1,'Here "fixed" describes a length that stays almost constant despite temperature, so it means unchanging in value.',37),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about a plain steel pendulum in a warming room?','Pendulum clocks keep better time than any earlier mechanism, but their accuracy hides a subtle problem: metals expand when warm. A steel rod lengthens as the temperature rises, and because a pendulum''s swing slows as it grows longer, a summer clock loses time that a winter clock did not lose. Around 1726 the English clockmaker John Harrison solved this with the gridiron pendulum, an assembly of alternating brass and steel rods joined to one bob. Brass expands more than steel, so the brass rods push the bob downward while the steel rods pull it upward. Adjusted in the right proportions, the two effects cancel, and the distance from the pivot to the bob stays almost fixed as the room warms or cools. The gridiron did not make clocks perfect, but by trading one kind of error for its opposite it let a household timepiece run for weeks without the daily correction that plainer pendulums demanded.','NONE','["It keeps exactly the same rate it kept in winter.","Its brass rods begin to lengthen on their own.","Its rod lengthens, so the clock gradually runs slow.","It stops swinging once the temperature rises."]','C',1,'With only one steel rod and no compensating brass, the rod lengthens and the swing slows, so the clock loses time.',38),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why does the passage mention apprentices and civic privileges?','Craft guilds in medieval towns are often remembered for guarding their recipes, and the habit had real economic logic. A guild''s income depended on a craft being harder to learn than it looked, so a glassmaker who revealed the exact mix of ashes and sand risked undercutting every rival in town. Rules on disclosure were therefore collective: an apprentice swore not to copy a formula into a book a stranger could buy, and a full member who trained an outsider''s son could be fined by the guild court. Yet secrecy cut both ways. Keeping a technique rare helped the few families who held it, but it also slowed the diffusion of useful methods across towns, so a clever dye or a stronger kiln might vanish when its last practitioner died. Historians now argue that the guild system traded broad technological spread for narrow price protection, and that the shift came only when patents promised inventors a timed reward and open publication in place of permanent silence.','NONE','["To give examples of the rewards guilds offered to inventive members.","To describe the daily routine of a medieval glass workshop.","To explain how patents were first granted to urban craftsmen.","To show the social and economic pressures that made secrecy rational."]','D',1,'The passage cites apprentices, guild courts, and family income to explain why members were pushed to keep recipes secret.',39),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "undercutting" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Craft guilds in medieval towns are often remembered for guarding their recipes, and the habit had real economic logic. A guild''s income depended on a craft being harder to learn than it looked, so a glassmaker who revealed the exact mix of ashes and sand risked undercutting every rival in town. Rules on disclosure were therefore collective: an apprentice swore not to copy a formula into a book a stranger could buy, and a full member who trained an outsider''s son could be fined by the guild court. Yet secrecy cut both ways. Keeping a technique rare helped the few families who held it, but it also slowed the diffusion of useful methods across towns, so a clever dye or a stronger kiln might vanish when its last practitioner died. Historians now argue that the guild system traded broad technological spread for narrow price protection, and that the shift came only when patents promised inventors a timed reward and open publication in place of permanent silence.','NONE','["helping to set up","damaging the position of","copying openly from","negotiating a deal with"]','B',1,'Revealing a recipe erodes a rival''s market advantage, so "undercutting" means damaging the position of.',40),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about the arrival of the patent system?','Craft guilds in medieval towns are often remembered for guarding their recipes, and the habit had real economic logic. A guild''s income depended on a craft being harder to learn than it looked, so a glassmaker who revealed the exact mix of ashes and sand risked undercutting every rival in town. Rules on disclosure were therefore collective: an apprentice swore not to copy a formula into a book a stranger could buy, and a full member who trained an outsider''s son could be fined by the guild court. Yet secrecy cut both ways. Keeping a technique rare helped the few families who held it, but it also slowed the diffusion of useful methods across towns, so a clever dye or a stronger kiln might vanish when its last practitioner died. Historians now argue that the guild system traded broad technological spread for narrow price protection, and that the shift came only when patents promised inventors a timed reward and open publication in place of permanent silence.','NONE','["It made guild secrets cheaper to buy.","It let an inventor keep a recipe secret forever.","It increased the number of guild courts in towns.","It offered protection strong enough to replace permanent secrecy."]','D',1,'The passage says the shift to openness came only when patents offered a timed reward, so patent protection was strong enough to replace secrecy.',41),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why are mangrove forests called natural coastal barriers?','Coastal engineering once meant pouring concrete, but a growing number of planners are looking instead at mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that fringe tropical shores. Mangroves defend a coastline in two ways. Their dense, submerged roots spread a rolling wave into many small ripples, and the friction between root and water drains a storm surge of speed and height, so the water that reaches a village arrives as a shallow sheet rather than a hammering wall. The same roots also act as a trap for sediment, slowing the current until suspended mud falls out and the shoreline builds seaward on its own, a self-repair a rigid seawall can never manage. That said, a mangrove is not a universal solution. It needs a gently sloping shore and a steady supply of fresh water upstream, so clearing a river for a dam can starve the trees and let erosion undo years of protection in a single storm season.','NONE','["Because their roots weaken incoming waves and build up sediment.","Because they grow tallest on the most exposed coastlines.","Because their concrete-like trunks block every storm surge.","Because they raise the height of an incoming wave."]','A',1,'The passage says mangroves both dampen a wave through root friction and trap sediment to build the shore seaward.',42),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "starve" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Coastal engineering once meant pouring concrete, but a growing number of planners are looking instead at mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that fringe tropical shores. Mangroves defend a coastline in two ways. Their dense, submerged roots spread a rolling wave into many small ripples, and the friction between root and water drains a storm surge of speed and height, so the water that reaches a village arrives as a shallow sheet rather than a hammering wall. The same roots also act as a trap for sediment, slowing the current until suspended mud falls out and the shoreline builds seaward on its own, a self-repair a rigid seawall can never manage. That said, a mangrove is not a universal solution. It needs a gently sloping shore and a steady supply of fresh water upstream, so clearing a river for a dam can starve the trees and let erosion undo years of protection in a single storm season.','NONE','["to deprive of what is needed","to cover with soil","to heat to a high temperature","to harvest on a schedule"]','A',1,'A dam cuts off the fresh water the trees require, so "starve" here means to deprive of what is needed.',43),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about rigid seawalls?','Coastal engineering once meant pouring concrete, but a growing number of planners are looking instead at mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that fringe tropical shores. Mangroves defend a coastline in two ways. Their dense, submerged roots spread a rolling wave into many small ripples, and the friction between root and water drains a storm surge of speed and height, so the water that reaches a village arrives as a shallow sheet rather than a hammering wall. The same roots also act as a trap for sediment, slowing the current until suspended mud falls out and the shoreline builds seaward on its own, a self-repair a rigid seawall can never manage. That said, a mangrove is not a universal solution. It needs a gently sloping shore and a steady supply of fresh water upstream, so clearing a river for a dam can starve the trees and let erosion undo years of protection in a single storm season.','NONE','["They can add new land after every major storm.","They slow a wave but still cannot rebuild the shoreline.","They trap more sediment than mangrove roots do.","They need far more fresh water than a mangrove."]','B',1,'The passage calls mangrove shoreline-building a repair a rigid seawall cannot manage, implying a wall may slow a wave but will not rebuild the coast.',44),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the main purpose of the passage?','When Napoleon''s soldiers found a black stone near the town of Rosetta in 1799, they did not know they held a key. The stone carries one decree in three scripts: Egyptian hieroglyphs at the top, a cursive Demotic section in the middle, and ancient Greek at the bottom. Because scholars could already read Greek, the parallel text let them match known words to unknown signs, and the same passage repeated three times meant a guess could be tested against two independent versions. Two men raced to crack the code. Thomas Young treated the hieroglyphs as a partly symbolic code and decoded a royal name, but he doubted the signs carried any sound. Jean-Francois Champollion, fluent in Coptic, argued the script recorded sounds and, in 1822, announced a working alphabet. Their rivalry shows how a single object can move a whole field forward, though the harder lesson is one of luck: without the Greek text, the other two scripts might have stayed unread for centuries.','NONE','["To compare two scholars who solved the stone and to stress the value of the Greek text.","To prove that hieroglyphs were never really understood.","To describe the daily life of priests who wrote the decree.","To explain why Napoleon''s army was defeated in Egypt."]','A',1,'The passage contrasts Young and Champollion and argues the bilingual Greek section was essential to the decipherment.',45),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "match" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','When Napoleon''s soldiers found a black stone near the town of Rosetta in 1799, they did not know they held a key. The stone carries one decree in three scripts: Egyptian hieroglyphs at the top, a cursive Demotic section in the middle, and ancient Greek at the bottom. Because scholars could already read Greek, the parallel text let them match known words to unknown signs, and the same passage repeated three times meant a guess could be tested against two independent versions. Two men raced to crack the code. Thomas Young treated the hieroglyphs as a partly symbolic code and decoded a royal name, but he doubted the signs carried any sound. Jean-Francois Champollion, fluent in Coptic, argued the script recorded sounds and, in 1822, announced a working alphabet. Their rivalry shows how a single object can move a whole field forward, though the harder lesson is one of luck: without the Greek text, the other two scripts might have stayed unread for centuries.','NONE','["to couple or line up with","to erase from memory","to sell at a price","to guess at random about"]','A',1,'Scholars paired known Greek words with unknown signs, so "match" means to couple or line up with.',46),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about Thomas Young''s approach?','When Napoleon''s soldiers found a black stone near the town of Rosetta in 1799, they did not know they held a key. The stone carries one decree in three scripts: Egyptian hieroglyphs at the top, a cursive Demotic section in the middle, and ancient Greek at the bottom. Because scholars could already read Greek, the parallel text let them match known words to unknown signs, and the same passage repeated three times meant a guess could be tested against two independent versions. Two men raced to crack the code. Thomas Young treated the hieroglyphs as a partly symbolic code and decoded a royal name, but he doubted the signs carried any sound. Jean-Francois Champollion, fluent in Coptic, argued the script recorded sounds and, in 1822, announced a working alphabet. Their rivalry shows how a single object can move a whole field forward, though the harder lesson is one of luck: without the Greek text, the other two scripts might have stayed unread for centuries.','NONE','["He believed the whole script was purely phonetic.","He could read every one of the stone''s three scripts.","He stopped too early by treating the signs as mainly ideas rather than sounds.","He refused to use the Greek text at all."]','C',1,'The passage says Young decoded a name but doubted the signs carried sound, so he stalled by seeing the script as symbolic.',47),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the main idea of the passage?','A shift nurse finishing at dawn feels the exhaustion the body was built to expect, but the reason is simpler than anyone admits. Nearly every cell runs on a circadian clock, a roughly twenty-four-hour cycle tuned to light and dark, which times hormone release, body temperature, and alertness. Daylight resets that clock each morning, yet a worker who sleeps through it receives the signal late or not at all. The consequences show up as drift rather than collapse: digestion, mood, and reaction speed slip out of step, and long-term studies have linked years of night work to higher rates of metabolic trouble. Trying to fix the clock with a nap often backfires, because the sleep debt lingers. Chronobiologists therefore argue that the practical answer is not a stronger will but a smarter schedule, with forward-rotating shifts and timed bright light, so the body''s clock can follow the calendar instead of resisting it.','NONE','["A single all-nighter permanently destroys the human body clock.","Rigid night shifts clash with a light-driven internal clock, so better scheduling is the fix.","Naps reliably reset the body''s hormonal rhythm.","Mood problems at work are unrelated to sleep timing."]','B',1,'The passage explains that night work conflicts with the light-tuned circadian clock and closes by advocating smarter scheduling and timed light.',48),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "drift" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','A shift nurse finishing at dawn feels the exhaustion the body was built to expect, but the reason is simpler than anyone admits. Nearly every cell runs on a circadian clock, a roughly twenty-four-hour cycle tuned to light and dark, which times hormone release, body temperature, and alertness. Daylight resets that clock each morning, yet a worker who sleeps through it receives the signal late or not at all. The consequences show up as drift rather than collapse: digestion, mood, and reaction speed slip out of step, and long-term studies have linked years of night work to higher rates of metabolic trouble. Trying to fix the clock with a nap often backfires, because the sleep debt lingers. Chronobiologists therefore argue that the practical answer is not a stronger will but a smarter schedule, with forward-rotating shifts and timed bright light, so the body''s clock can follow the calendar instead of resisting it.','NONE','["a sudden total failure","a deliberate correction","a gradual misalignment","a welcome improvement"]','C',1,'The author contrasts drift with collapse, describing processes slowly slipping out of step, so "drift" means a gradual misalignment.',49),
(@set07_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about a "forward-rotating" shift?','A shift nurse finishing at dawn feels the exhaustion the body was built to expect, but the reason is simpler than anyone admits. Nearly every cell runs on a circadian clock, a roughly twenty-four-hour cycle tuned to light and dark, which times hormone release, body temperature, and alertness. Daylight resets that clock each morning, yet a worker who sleeps through it receives the signal late or not at all. The consequences show up as drift rather than collapse: digestion, mood, and reaction speed slip out of step, and long-term studies have linked years of night work to higher rates of metabolic trouble. Trying to fix the clock with a nap often backfires, because the sleep debt lingers. Chronobiologists therefore argue that the practical answer is not a stronger will but a smarter schedule, with forward-rotating shifts and timed bright light, so the body''s clock can follow the calendar instead of resisting it.','NONE','["It keeps the body clock permanently frozen at noon.","It moves start times later in step with the natural clock.","It relies only on long naps between shifts.","It removes the need for daylight altogether."]','B',1,'Because the passage ties circadian health to following the calendar, a forward-rotating shift most likely advances start times gradually with the body''s natural clock.',50);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Excuse me, is the maintenance desk open right now?','TTS','["It closes at six, so you caught it by a few minutes.","On the third shelf near the window.","Yes, a ticket costs three dollars.","I fixed the radiator myself last term."]','A',1,'A question about opening status is best answered by stating when it closes, so option A fits.',51),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do I need my room number to file a ticket?','TTS','["It''s on the second floor near the elevator.","Yes, they ask for it at the top of the form.","About forty minutes for the crew to arrive.","No, the ticket itself is free to submit."]','B',1,'A yes/no about what information is needed is best answered by confirming the requirement, so option B fits.',52),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you hold my key at the desk until the office opens tomorrow?','TTS','["The spare keys cost three dollars each.","It opens at ten on Monday only.","Sure, just sign for it when you pick it up.","Yes, it is already listed in the system."]','C',1,'A request to hold an item is best answered by agreeing and giving the pickup condition, so option C fits.',53),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How long does a radiator repair usually take?','TTS','["Yes, it''s in the basement near the boiler.","Room 210 down the hallway.","On the maintenance portal after hours.","Most jobs are finished within a single day."]','D',1,'A how-long question needs a duration, and only option D gives a typical timeframe.',54),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Sorry, do you know when the registrar counter opens?','TTS','["It opens at nine and closes at four.","You''ll need your student ID.","The form is already on the website.","I registered for two courses last week."]','A',1,'A question about a counter''s hours is best answered with those hours, so option A fits.',55),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Would you mind checking whether my enrollment hold is gone?','TTS','["No, I''ve never been to that office.","Let me pull up your record and see.","The hold form is downstairs on two.","Yes, the counter opens at nine thirty."]','B',1,'A request to look something up is best answered by agreeing to check, so option B fits.',56),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Is it still possible to add a course after the deadline?','TTS','["Yes, the deadline was last Friday.","The registrar is a very busy office.","Only with the instructor''s signature, but it can be done.","No new course is allowed at this point."]','C',1,'A yes/no about a possibility is best answered by stating the condition, so option C fits.',57),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Where do I turn in my graduation application?','TTS','["It costs nothing to apply.","I plan to graduate in the spring.","Once per term is the limit.","The clerk at the front counter takes those."]','D',1,'A where question is best answered by naming the person or office, so option D fits.',58),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you think I have enough credits to declare a minor?','TTS','["You''re close, but one math course is still missing.","That minor is very popular this year.","Yes, I''m going to declare one too.","The application form is by the door."]','A',1,'A question about one''s own status is best answered by describing that status, so option A fits.',59),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you explain how the course waitlist works?','TTS','["Yes, I already joined that list.","Students are called in the order they signed up.","The waitlist desk is around the corner.","The list has about twenty names on it."]','B',1,'A how-does-it-work question is best answered by describing the process, so option B fits.',60),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','I''m trying to reach my seminar partner about the reading.','TTS','["The seminar meets on Thursday afternoon.","The assigned reading was quite short.","Her extension is listed on the syllabus.","I already finished the reading on my own."]','C',1,'A statement about contacting someone is best answered by supplying the means, so option C fits.',61),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Have you finished the article for the seminar yet?','TTS','["Yes, the seminar starts at two.","The article was due last Friday.","I lent you my copy of the article.","Almost, but my last two pages are still messy."]','D',1,'A have-you-done-it question is best answered by reporting one''s own progress, so option D fits.',62),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Is this the line for seminar room check-in?','TTS','["It is, though the list opens in a minute.","The seminar room is up the stairs.","Yes, I brought my annotated article.","No, the class is already full."]','A',1,'Confirming a line''s purpose needs a yes plus a note, so option A fits.',63),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Excuse me, where can I buy a museum map?','TTS','["The second-floor gallery sells postcards.","The gift shop by the entrance has them.","The maps don''t cost very much.","I visited this museum last spring."]','B',1,'A where-to-buy question is best answered by naming the place, so option B fits.',64),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Can you print a photo from the exhibit for me?','TTS','["The photograph is rather old.","Yes, I''ll take your print order.","We only sell the printed reproductions at the counter.","Personal cameras aren''t allowed in here."]','C',1,'A can-you request is best answered by stating what the shop offers, so option C fits.',65),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How much is the student discount at this shop?','TTS','["No, I''m a full-time student here.","Students over twelve enter for free.","I''m only looking for a discount.","It takes two dollars off any purchase."]','D',1,'A how-much question needs an amount, and only option D gives the size of the discount.',66),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do these buses still run after the last train tonight?','TTS','["The last one leaves about ten before midnight.","Yes, I take this bus every day.","The train station is across the street.","Midnight is quite late to travel."]','A',1,'A whether-the-service-exists question is best answered with the timing fact, so option A fits.',67),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Sorry, is the campus shuttle free for visitors?','TTS','["Yes, the shuttle leaves on the hour.","It is, no ticket or fare is needed.","That shuttle stops at the train station.","The late shuttle is always very crowded."]','B',1,'A yes/no about cost is best answered by confirming the price, so option B confirms it''s free.',68),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I signed up for the statistics section, but the registrar page still shows me as not enrolled. — Woman: Let me see. Your tuition hold was cleared this morning, but the system only refreshes enrollment once a day, overnight. — Student: So if I check tomorrow it should be right? — Woman: Yes, and if it''s still missing by Thursday, come back and we''ll fix it by hand.','TTS','["The enrollment record updates only once each day.","The tuition hold has not been cleared at all.","The student must re-register for the course.","The statistics section has reached its cap."]','A',1,'The woman explains that enrollment refreshes only once a day, naming the reason the record still looks wrong.',69),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I signed up for the statistics section, but the registrar page still shows me as not enrolled. — Woman: Let me see. Your tuition hold was cleared this morning, but the system only refreshes enrollment once a day, overnight. — Student: So if I check tomorrow it should be right? — Woman: Yes, and if it''s still missing by Thursday, come back and we''ll fix it by hand.','TTS','["To pay the outstanding tuition fee.","To ask for a different section.","To have the enrollment corrected if it is still wrong.","To confirm her registration by phone."]','C',1,'The woman says to return if the record is still missing Thursday, so the visit would be to fix it by hand.',70),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I signed up for the statistics section, but the registrar page still shows me as not enrolled. — Woman: Let me see. Your tuition hold was cleared this morning, but the system only refreshes enrollment once a day, overnight. — Student: So if I check tomorrow it should be right? — Woman: Yes, and if it''s still missing by Thursday, come back and we''ll fix it by hand.','TTS','["This afternoon.","On Thursday morning.","Within one hour.","After the overnight update."]','D',1,'Enrollment refreshes overnight, so the record should first show the course after that update.',71),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Professor: Your seminar paper needs a central question, not just a summary of the sources. — Student: I have four sources that all describe the same policy. — Professor: Then ask what they disagree about and make that your question. Bring a one-page outline by Monday so we can narrow it before you draft.','TTS','["Add more sources to the paper.","Turn the disagreement among the sources into the central question.","Summarize all four sources completely.","Postpone the draft until Monday."]','B',1,'The professor advises making the sources'' disagreement the central question, so option B is the advice given.',72),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Professor: Your seminar paper needs a central question, not just a summary of the sources. — Student: I have four sources that all describe the same policy. — Professor: Then ask what they disagree about and make that your question. Bring a one-page outline by Monday so we can narrow it before you draft.','TTS','["A short one-page outline.","The finished draft of the paper.","Four photocopies of the sources.","An entirely new source list."]','A',1,'The professor asks for a one-page outline by Monday, so that is what the student must bring.',73),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Professor: Your seminar paper needs a central question, not just a summary of the sources. — Student: I have four sources that all describe the same policy. — Professor: Then ask what they disagree about and make that your question. Bring a one-page outline by Monday so we can narrow it before you draft.','TTS','["It uses too few sources.","It is longer than the limit allows.","It reports the sources without asking a question.","It cites the wrong policy altogether."]','C',1,'The professor says a summary is not enough, so the paper''s problem is that it reports sources without a central question.',74),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Is this the last stop before the station? — Man: It is, but the express doesn''t halt here. The next express arrives in twenty minutes and drops you right at the platform. — Woman: Will that still get me onto the nine o''clock train? — Man: You''ll have about fifteen minutes to spare.','TTS','["The local bus stops here but the express does not.","The train she needs has already left.","The station is closed for the weekend.","Her ticket is for the wrong day."]','A',1,'The man explains that the express does not halt at this stop, so her problem is catching it elsewhere; option A states the mismatch.',75),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Is this the last stop before the station? — Man: It is, but the express doesn''t halt here. The next express arrives in twenty minutes and drops you right at the platform. — Woman: Will that still get me onto the nine o''clock train? — Man: You''ll have about fifteen minutes to spare.','TTS','["By walking from this stop.","On the next local bus.","By asking the driver to halt here.","By taking the express that arrives in twenty minutes."]','D',1,'The man says the next express in twenty minutes goes straight to the platform, so that is how she will reach the station.',76),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Is this the last stop before the station? — Man: It is, but the express doesn''t halt here. The next express arrives in twenty minutes and drops you right at the platform. — Woman: Will that still get me onto the nine o''clock train? — Man: You''ll have about fifteen minutes to spare.','TTS','["She will have a small but safe margin before the train.","She is certain to miss the train.","The nine o''clock train will itself be delayed.","The next express is already full."]','A',1,'The man says she will have about fifteen minutes to spare, so she will have a small but adequate margin.',77),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Thank you for seeing me about the front-desk job. — Man: I reviewed your application. The shift starts at seven, and some Saturdays are required. Does that fit your classes? — Student: Mornings are fine, but I have a class every other Saturday. — Man: Then let''s try three shifts a week and swap Saturdays with another student when you can.','TTS','["The hourly pay for the position.","How long the interview will last.","Whether the applicant''s schedule fits the job hours.","Where the service desk is located."]','C',1,'The whole exchange turns on matching the applicant''s availability to the required shifts, so option C is the concern.',78),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Thank you for seeing me about the front-desk job. — Man: I reviewed your application. The shift starts at seven, and some Saturdays are required. Does that fit your classes? — Student: Mornings are fine, but I have a class every other Saturday. — Man: Then let''s try three shifts a week and swap Saturdays with another student when you can.','TTS','["Assigning her only the morning shift.","Giving her a different job entirely.","Asking her to drop the Saturday class.","A reduced weekly schedule with swapped Saturday shifts."]','D',1,'The man proposes three shifts a week and swapping Saturdays when she has class, so option D is the solution.',79),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Thank you for seeing me about the front-desk job. — Man: I reviewed your application. The shift starts at seven, and some Saturdays are required. Does that fit your classes? — Student: Mornings are fine, but I have a class every other Saturday. — Man: Then let''s try three shifts a week and swap Saturdays with another student when you can.','TTS','["A shift at the front desk.","A class.","An evening exam.","A required seminar."]','B',1,'The student says she has a class every other Saturday, so that is what conflicts.',80),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention Hall C residents. Hot water will be shut off tomorrow from nine in the morning until three in the afternoon so a heating valve can be replaced. Showers on the ground floor stay working the whole time, and any urgent plumbing problem should be reported to the maintenance desk rather than the front office. Service resumes automatically in the evening.','TTS','["A planned interruption of hot water in one hall.","A new maintenance fee for students.","The permanent closing of the hall showers.","A weekend elevator inspection."]','A',1,'The announcement describes a hot-water shutdown from nine to three for a valve repair.',81),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention Hall C residents. Hot water will be shut off tomorrow from nine in the morning until three in the afternoon so a heating valve can be replaced. Showers on the ground floor stay working the whole time, and any urgent plumbing problem should be reported to the maintenance desk rather than the front office. Service resumes automatically in the evening.','TTS','["In the front office.","At the maintenance desk.","On the ground floor.","Only back in the evening."]','C',1,'The notice says ground-floor showers stay working during the shutdown.',82),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention Hall C residents. Hot water will be shut off tomorrow from nine in the morning until three in the afternoon so a heating valve can be replaced. Showers on the ground floor stay working the whole time, and any urgent plumbing problem should be reported to the maintenance desk rather than the front office. Service resumes automatically in the evening.','TTS','["Wait until service resumes.","Report it to the maintenance desk.","Handle it at the front office.","Turn off the main valve alone."]','B',1,'Urgent plumbing issues are directed to the maintenance desk, not the front office.',83),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Good morning, visitors. The museum shop will open one hour early today, at eight rather than nine, because a traveling exhibit arrives this afternoon. For that exhibit the photography licence and the printed catalog are both discounted by a third until closing. Normal hours return tomorrow.','TTS','["It closes one hour earlier.","It stops selling catalogs.","It charges a new licence fee.","It opens earlier than usual."]','D',1,'The shop opens at eight instead of nine today, i.e. earlier than usual.',84),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Good morning, visitors. The museum shop will open one hour early today, at eight rather than nine, because a traveling exhibit arrives this afternoon. For that exhibit the photography licence and the printed catalog are both discounted by a third until closing. Normal hours return tomorrow.','TTS','["The printed catalog and the photography licence.","Only the entrance ticket.","The ground-floor guided tours.","Next week''s admission pass."]','A',1,'The announcement discounts the photography licence and the printed catalog for the new exhibit.',85),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Good morning, visitors. The museum shop will open one hour early today, at eight rather than nine, because a traveling exhibit arrives this afternoon. For that exhibit the photography licence and the printed catalog are both discounted by a third until closing. Normal hours return tomorrow.','TTS','["Because it is a public holiday.","Because staff are unavailable.","Because a special exhibit is being installed later that day.","Because the museum is closing permanently."]','C',1,'The early opening is tied to a traveling exhibit arriving that afternoon.',86),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Reminder for the Thursday seminar. Please bring your annotated article, because the discussion will be graded on the margin notes rather than on a summary. The reading room is reserved until six so you can photocopy a missing page. Anyone who has not received the handout should email me before class and I will bring extra copies.','TTS','["By a written essay.","By the notes written in the article.","By an oral quiz.","By attendance alone."]','B',1,'The seminar is graded on margin notes, not on a summary or a test.',87),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Reminder for the Thursday seminar. Please bring your annotated article, because the discussion will be graded on the margin notes rather than on a summary. The reading room is reserved until six so you can photocopy a missing page. Anyone who has not received the handout should email me before class and I will bring extra copies.','TTS','["Photocopy it in the reading room.","Arrive early and borrow one.","Skip the seminar that week.","Email the instructor before class."]','D',1,'Students without the handout are told to email the instructor, who will bring extra copies.',88),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Reminder for the Thursday seminar. Please bring your annotated article, because the discussion will be graded on the margin notes rather than on a summary. The reading room is reserved until six so you can photocopy a missing page. Anyone who has not received the handout should email me before class and I will bring extra copies.','TTS','["Six o''clock.","Noon.","Ten in the morning.","Eight o''clock."]','A',1,'The reading room is reserved until six for photocopying.',89),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: So let us ask what actually keeps a factory worker alert at three in the morning. The body runs on an internal clock, the circadian rhythm, which is set mainly by daylight reaching the eye. That clock expects bright days and dark nights, releasing alerting chemicals in the morning and sleep-promoting ones after dusk. A night-shift worker fights that schedule: bright lights at home confuse the clock, and a dark commute tells the brain it is already bedtime. Researchers therefore look for ways to nudge the rhythm, using bright lamps at the start of a shift and sunglasses on the way home, so the internal clock drifts only slightly rather than flipping. The results are modest but real: workers who manage their light exposure report better focus and fewer errors, and it matters because the risk of industrial accidents climbs sharply in the last hours of a night shift.','TTS','["How the internal body clock affects night-shift workers and how light can help.","The best overtime pay for shift workers.","Why factories should simply close at night.","The invention of the modern electric lightbulb."]','A',1,'The talk explains how light sets the circadian clock and how managing light helps night-shift workers.',90),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: So let us ask what actually keeps a factory worker alert at three in the morning. The body runs on an internal clock, the circadian rhythm, which is set mainly by daylight reaching the eye. That clock expects bright days and dark nights, releasing alerting chemicals in the morning and sleep-promoting ones after dusk. A night-shift worker fights that schedule: bright lights at home confuse the clock, and a dark commute tells the brain it is already bedtime. Researchers therefore look for ways to nudge the rhythm, using bright lamps at the start of a shift and sunglasses on the way home, so the internal clock drifts only slightly rather than flipping. The results are modest but real: workers who manage their light exposure report better focus and fewer errors, and it matters because the risk of industrial accidents climbs sharply in the last hours of a night shift.','TTS','["The temperature of the room.","The sound of the machinery.","Daylight reaching the eye.","The worker''s mealtimes."]','C',1,'The professor says the circadian clock is set mainly by daylight reaching the eye.',91),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: So let us ask what actually keeps a factory worker alert at three in the morning. The body runs on an internal clock, the circadian rhythm, which is set mainly by daylight reaching the eye. That clock expects bright days and dark nights, releasing alerting chemicals in the morning and sleep-promoting ones after dusk. A night-shift worker fights that schedule: bright lights at home confuse the clock, and a dark commute tells the brain it is already bedtime. Researchers therefore look for ways to nudge the rhythm, using bright lamps at the start of a shift and sunglasses on the way home, so the internal clock drifts only slightly rather than flipping. The results are modest but real: workers who manage their light exposure report better focus and fewer errors, and it matters because the risk of industrial accidents climbs sharply in the last hours of a night shift.','TTS','["It raises the cost of the trip.","It overstimulates the brain.","It has no effect on the clock.","It signals the brain to start preparing for sleep."]','D',1,'A dark commute tells the brain it is bedtime, so it triggers sleep preparation.',92),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: So let us ask what actually keeps a factory worker alert at three in the morning. The body runs on an internal clock, the circadian rhythm, which is set mainly by daylight reaching the eye. That clock expects bright days and dark nights, releasing alerting chemicals in the morning and sleep-promoting ones after dusk. A night-shift worker fights that schedule: bright lights at home confuse the clock, and a dark commute tells the brain it is already bedtime. Researchers therefore look for ways to nudge the rhythm, using bright lamps at the start of a shift and sunglasses on the way home, so the internal clock drifts only slightly rather than flipping. The results are modest but real: workers who manage their light exposure report better focus and fewer errors, and it matters because the risk of industrial accidents climbs sharply in the last hours of a night shift.','TTS','["They prove nothing so far.","They are limited but show better focus and fewer errors.","They apply only to day-shift workers.","They show that workers should quit night shifts."]','B',1,'The professor calls the results modest but real, with better focus and fewer errors reported.',93),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: When I say a coast protects itself, I usually mean mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that grow where river water meets the sea. Their tangled roots do two jobs at once. First, they break the force of a storm surge, so a wave that would crack a seawall arrives inland as a shallow sheet of water. Second, the roots slow the current enough that drifting mud settles out and builds new land, which is why a healthy mangrove front often grows seaward over time. Concrete barriers do the first job and none of the second; they simply reflect wave energy, and that reflected energy digs holes in the sand beneath them. That is why engineers now study mangroves as living breakwaters. The catch is that a mangrove needs a gentle, sloping shore and the right salt balance, so it cannot just be planted on any coastline; it works only where the conditions it evolved in are left intact.','TTS','["Seawalls should replace every natural mangrove.","Mangroves grow only deep inside rivers.","Storm surges are becoming smaller each decade.","Mangroves protect coasts in two ways a concrete wall cannot fully copy."]','D',1,'The talk states mangroves both weaken waves and build land, two roles a seawall cannot fully copy, so option D captures the main point.',94),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: When I say a coast protects itself, I usually mean mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that grow where river water meets the sea. Their tangled roots do two jobs at once. First, they break the force of a storm surge, so a wave that would crack a seawall arrives inland as a shallow sheet of water. Second, the roots slow the current enough that drifting mud settles out and builds new land, which is why a healthy mangrove front often grows seaward over time. Concrete barriers do the first job and none of the second; they simply reflect wave energy, and that reflected energy digs holes in the sand beneath them. That is why engineers now study mangroves as living breakwaters. The catch is that a mangrove needs a gentle, sloping shore and the right salt balance, so it cannot just be planted on any coastline; it works only where the conditions it evolved in are left intact.','TTS','["It is washed straight back out to sea.","It hardens into solid rock.","It settles out and gradually creates new land.","It poisons the roots of the trees."]','C',1,'The professor says the roots slow the current until drifting mud settles out and builds new land.',95),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: When I say a coast protects itself, I usually mean mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that grow where river water meets the sea. Their tangled roots do two jobs at once. First, they break the force of a storm surge, so a wave that would crack a seawall arrives inland as a shallow sheet of water. Second, the roots slow the current enough that drifting mud settles out and builds new land, which is why a healthy mangrove front often grows seaward over time. Concrete barriers do the first job and none of the second; they simply reflect wave energy, and that reflected energy digs holes in the sand beneath them. That is why engineers now study mangroves as living breakwaters. The catch is that a mangrove needs a gentle, sloping shore and the right salt balance, so it cannot just be planted on any coastline; it works only where the conditions it evolved in are left intact.','TTS','["They grow far too slowly.","Reflected wave energy scours sand away beneath them.","They require a fresh-water balance to survive.","They cost nothing to build."]','B',1,'The professor explains that a wall reflects wave energy, and that reflected energy digs holes in the sand beneath it.',96),
(@set07_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the talk and answer the question.','Professor: When I say a coast protects itself, I usually mean mangroves, the salt-tolerant trees that grow where river water meets the sea. Their tangled roots do two jobs at once. First, they break the force of a storm surge, so a wave that would crack a seawall arrives inland as a shallow sheet of water. Second, the roots slow the current enough that drifting mud settles out and builds new land, which is why a healthy mangrove front often grows seaward over time. Concrete barriers do the first job and none of the second; they simply reflect wave energy, and that reflected energy digs holes in the sand beneath them. That is why engineers now study mangroves as living breakwaters. The catch is that a mangrove needs a gentle, sloping shore and the right salt balance, so it cannot just be planted on any coastline; it works only where the conditions it evolved in are left intact.','TTS','["They cannot survive direct sunlight.","They grow only after a major storm.","They are too tall for any low coast.","They need the specific shore and salt conditions they evolved in."]','D',1,'The closing point is that a mangrove works only where its needed gentle slope and salt balance are preserved.',97);
